WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Containers can simplify application dependencies,

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.495
but they do not remove host GPU driver requirements.

00:00:05.495 --> 00:00:09.500
The host still needs the right driver, kernel modules, device nodes, and permissions.

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.380
The container runtime then exposes GPU devices and compatible libraries into the

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:13.955
container.

00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:17.500
If the host stack is broken, the container cannot magically repair it.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.475
For NVIDIA systems,

00:00:18.475 --> 00:00:23.010
the NVIDIA Container Toolkit is commonly used at a high level to make GPUs available to

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:23.479
containers.

00:00:23.479 --> 00:00:27.820
ROCm container workflows have their own considerations around devices, groups, libraries,

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:28.995
and supported images.

00:00:28.995 --> 00:00:29.710
In both cases,

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:34.105
the container image must be compatible with the host driver and the runtime expectations

00:00:34.105 --> 00:00:35.685
of the AI application.

00:00:35.685 --> 00:00:39.960
Container failures often come from missing runtime configuration, missing device access,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:43.000
permissions, incorrect image selection, or version mismatch.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:47.110
A container might run successfully but still use the CPU if the application cannot see the

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.000
GPU libraries.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.500
Validation should happen inside the container, not only on the host.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:55.475
The question is whether the workload sees and uses acceleration where it actually runs.

00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:59.505
Good container practice includes documenting the host driver, container runtime settings,

00:00:59.505 --> 00:01:04.000
image tags, environment variables, mounted devices, and validation output.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Pin known-good images for stable systems.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:06.930
Update deliberately.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.500
Containers make AI applications easier to package,

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:14.000
but GPU access remains a contract between host driver, runtime configuration,

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:16.000
image contents, and application code.
